# Riverpod Patterns

## State Class (Freezed)

```dart
@freezed
abstract class UserState with _$UserState {
  const factory UserState({
    @Default(false) bool isLoading,
    @Default(null) User? user,
    @Default(null) String? error,
  }) = _UserState;
}
```

Many controllers skip dedicated state classes and use `AsyncValue<SimpleType>` directly.

## Controller (Riverpod Codegen)

```dart
// Simple return type
@riverpod
class UserController extends _$UserController {
  @override
  FutureOr<List<User>> build() async {
    return ref.read(userRepositoryProvider).getUsers();
  }
}

// With state class
@riverpod
class ProfileController extends _$ProfileController {
  @override
  FutureOr<ProfileState> build() async => const ProfileState();
}
```

Common `build()` return types: `FutureOr<bool>`, `FutureOr<List<T>>`, `FutureOr<T?>`, `FutureOr<String?>`.

## `@Riverpod(keepAlive: true)`

Use for controllers/providers that must survive navigation (not auto-disposed):
- Repository providers, service providers
- Global state controllers (e.g. auth, user session)

## State Access

| Method | Use Case |
|--------|----------|
| `ref.watch()` | Reactive state (rebuilds on change) |
| `ref.read()` | One-time access (in callbacks) |
| `ref.listen()` | Side effects (snackbars, navigation) |

## AsyncValue

Use `AsyncValue` for async operations:
- `AsyncData` — success state
- `AsyncLoading` — loading state
- `AsyncError` — error state
